There are only few quest monster types per role and kills on the quest 
do affect extinction on non quest areas, so killing enough quest 
monsters do affect post quest monster generation, but not very much as 
there are several other monsters to spawn.

> It may not even have been related to making anything less "easy" -- just
> a knowledge that when you restrict monster generation to only a few
> types, a player could easily extinct those types before reaching the
> bottom of the Quest, and it would seem silly to provide a built-in
> method of near-guaranteed extinction of a few monster types each game.

I'm quite sure that it's there for just that reason. My rationale:

1. Extincting monsters in non quest levels doesn't affect monster 
generation on quest eg. extinction limit does not prevent monster 
generation on quest levels.
2. Killing monsters on quest levels does affect monster generation on 
non-quest levels, eg. quest kills do increment extinction counter.

>>and that death drop is there
>>exactly for the reason to introduce random items to the game such a way
>>that the player can have the first opportunity to use them.
> 
> There's a difference here, though.  Death drops are there to introduce
> random items into the game.  But do you think the Quest was intended to
> be a limitless source of items?

Why code around extinction counter but not around death drops? As a 
systems analyst I'd say that it was deliberate action.

